# Generated state files are never merged, only regenerated.
#
# `state/DASHBOARD.md` embeds a reconciliation timestamp and the last ten
# commits, so it is *different on every branch by construction*. Textually
# merging it fails on every merge — the same conflict in `state/DASHBOARD.md` was
# resolved by hand on every one of the seven PRs in the 2.28.0 cycle.
#
# `merge=union` keeps both sides instead of conflicting. That is correct here and
# for one reason only: the file is a build artefact, and the post-commit hook
# regenerates it from the tree it lands on. The doubled lines exist for exactly
# one commit — the merge commit itself — and the hook's own auto-commit replaces
# the whole file immediately after.
#
# Measured, not assumed:
#
#   merge=ours   does NOT work from .gitattributes alone. Git needs a per-clone
#                `[merge "ours"] driver = true`, so a fresh clone would still
#                conflict — the fix would be silently absent exactly where it
#                matters.
#   merge=union  works, needs no configuration, and survives a clone.
#   gitignore    would also end the conflicts, but MACP's startup protocol
#                (AGENTS.md step 2) reads this file to judge staleness, so a
#                fresh clone must contain it.
#
# Trade-off accepted: for one commit the file carries two `Last Reconciled`
# lines. The protocol reads that line to decide trust, and a doubled line is
# still readable — the hook has already rewritten it by the time anything else
# runs.

state/DASHBOARD.md merge=union
